As the user types, Ladybird checks the text around the caret: the
paragraph it’s in, clipped to 1024 code units around it. But the word
still being typed is exempt: Its markers come off, and no check flags
it, until the caret leaves it.

Blink’s HotModeSpellCheckRequester::CheckSpellingAt() treats a
half-typed word the same way: It takes the markers off the word that
CurrentWordIfTypingInPartialWord() finds, and checks nothing while the
user is still typing it. Otherwise, it checks the caret’s paragraph, or
the kHotModeChunkSize (1024) characters of it centered on the caret.

WebKit’s Editor::editorUIUpdateTimerFired() does likewise: It clears the
markers around a new selection, then checks the words that were around
the old one once the caret has moved off them.
